WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.213
Here's a question that sounds too simple.

00:00:03.213 --> 00:00:06.340
How does a computer understand the number 8?

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.663
Inside, there is no digit 8 at all.

00:00:09.663 --> 00:00:17.477
Deep down, everything in a computer, numbers, text, images, and the instructions themselves,

00:00:17.477 --> 00:00:20.450
is stored as patterns of 0s and 1s.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.652
The number 8 is stored as 1, 0, 0, 0.

00:00:24.652 --> 00:00:28.920
By the end of this part, you'll know why.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.980
This is Lecture 1, part 2.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:36.703
We'll climb the ladder of languages from machine code up to Python,

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:38.987
see what the operating system does,

00:00:38.987 --> 00:00:44.730
and then meet the number systems computers use: binary, octal, decimal, and hexadecimal.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.227
The only language a CPU runs directly is machine code:

00:00:49.227 --> 00:00:54.404
instructions written as binary numbers, and different for each kind of CPU.

00:00:54.404 --> 00:00:56.820
Nobody wants to write that by hand.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.209
So the first step up is assembly language.

00:01:00.209 --> 00:01:05.202
It gives each machine instruction a short name, like 'add' or 'load'.

00:01:05.202 --> 00:01:08.530
Assembly and machine code match almost 1 to 1.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.386
A program called an assembler translates the assembly source into machine code.

00:01:14.386 --> 00:01:18.149
But each assembly language belongs to one kind of CPU.

00:01:18.149 --> 00:01:22.400
A program written for an Intel chip won't run on an ARM chip.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.198
High-level languages go further.

00:01:25.198 --> 00:01:27.264
You write 'result = 2 + 3',

00:01:27.264 --> 00:01:30.630
and never mention registers or memory cells.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:35.881
C came from Dennis Ritchie at Bell Labs, between 1969 and 1973.

00:01:35.881 --> 00:01:38.993
It's close to the machine and very fast,

00:01:38.993 --> 00:01:42.260
and it's still used for operating systems.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:46.984
C++, from Bjarne Stroustrup, started in 1979.

00:01:46.984 --> 00:01:50.970
It adds object-oriented programming to C.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:57.258
Java, from James Gosling at Sun Microsystems, came out in 1995.

00:01:57.258 --> 00:02:00.840
Its promise: write once, run anywhere.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:05.786
And Python, from Guido van Rossum, was released in 1991.

00:02:05.786 --> 00:02:09.069
It's open source, it has huge libraries,

00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:13.830
and it's the language of this course, and of most AI work.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.438
A high-level language can't run directly.

00:02:17.438 --> 00:02:24.499
It must be translated first: by a compiler, which translates the whole program ahead of time, as in C,

00:02:24.499 --> 00:02:29.760
or by an interpreter, which translates and runs it as it goes, as in Python.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:36.245
Here's the same computation at 4 levels: Python, C, assembly, and machine code.

00:02:36.245 --> 00:02:42.070
Each line down is closer to the machine, and further from how people think.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.166
That gives a trade-off.

00:02:44.166 --> 00:02:50.164
Lower-level languages have higher language efficiency: the programs run faster.

00:02:50.164 --> 00:02:58.060
Higher-level languages have higher development efficiency: programs are quicker to write, read, and fix.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:02.764
For most programs, your time costs more than the computer's.

00:03:02.764 --> 00:03:07.347
And when speed really matters, Python calls libraries written in C.

00:03:07.347 --> 00:03:10.220
That's how Python's AI libraries run fast.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:13.707
So Python isn't a bad language because it's slower.

00:03:13.707 --> 00:03:15.922
It just makes a different trade-off.

00:03:15.922 --> 00:03:17.890
Choose the language for the job.

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:24.404
One more program sits between your code and the hardware: the operating system.

00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:30.650
It controls the computer's activities, shares out memory, CPU time, files and devices,

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.627
and decides which program runs, and when.

00:03:33.977 --> 00:03:36.818
Applications are built on top of it.

00:03:36.818 --> 00:03:42.028
Windows, macOS, Linux, iOS, and Android are all operating systems.

00:03:42.028 --> 00:03:48.737
When your Python program prints to the screen, it asks the operating system to do it.

00:03:49.087 --> 00:03:53.461
Step back, and the lecture so far is 3 ladders of abstraction.

00:03:53.461 --> 00:03:56.847
Each level hides the details of the level below.

00:03:57.197 --> 00:04:01.229
Languages: machine code, assembly, C, Python.

00:04:01.229 --> 00:04:06.068
Systems: hardware, the operating system, applications.

00:04:06.068 --> 00:04:13.237
And data: binary digits, then numbers and text, then data structures like lists.

00:04:13.587 --> 00:04:17.522
When you write Python, you stand on top of all three.

00:04:17.522 --> 00:04:22.867
Now let's climb down the data ladder, to its bottom rung: binary digits.

00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:26.724
We write numbers in positional notation.

00:04:26.724 --> 00:04:30.315
A positional number system has 2 parts: a base,

00:04:30.315 --> 00:04:34.747
and 1 symbol for each value from 0 up to the base minus 1.

00:04:35.097 --> 00:04:38.908
Decimal has base 10, and the symbols 0 to 9.

00:04:38.908 --> 00:04:43.499
When counting passes 9, we don't invent a new symbol.

00:04:43.499 --> 00:04:46.097
We write 1, 0. On 10, carry 1.

00:04:46.447 --> 00:04:52.154
And every number is a sum: each digit times the weight of its position.

00:04:52.154 --> 00:04:54.967
The weights are powers of the base.

00:04:55.567 --> 00:04:56.804
Take 472.5.

00:04:56.804 --> 00:05:05.687
It's 4 times 10 squared, plus 7 times 10, plus 2 times 1, plus 5 times a tenth.

00:05:06.037 --> 00:05:10.197
To the left of the point, the weights go 1, 10, 100.

00:05:10.197 --> 00:05:13.397
To the right, a tenth, then a hundredth.

00:05:14.167 --> 00:05:20.403
In binary, the base is 2, and there are only 2 symbols: 0 and 1.

00:05:20.403 --> 00:05:21.767
On 2, carry 1.

00:05:22.117 --> 00:05:27.621
The weights are powers of 2: 1, 2, 4, 8, and so on.

00:05:27.621 --> 00:05:32.586
So 1, 0, 0, 1 in binary is 1 eight plus 1 one.

00:05:32.586 --> 00:05:33.557
That's 9.

00:05:33.907 --> 00:05:46.747
Counting in binary goes: 0, 1, 10, 11, 100, 101, 110, 111, 1000.

00:05:46.997 --> 00:05:53.105
Each time a position reaches 2, it resets to 0 and carries 1 to the left,

00:05:53.105 --> 00:05:56.117
just as 9 plus 1 carries in decimal.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:59.733
Pause the video and predict.

00:05:59.733 --> 00:06:03.070
In binary, what comes after 1011?

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:05.700
And what comes after 1111?

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:08.463
1011 plus 1 is 1100.

00:06:08.463 --> 00:06:16.650
The last digit carries, the next one carries too, and the third becomes 1.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:21.000
And 1111 plus 1 is 10000.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:27.880
Every digit carries, just like 9999 plus 1.

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:31.408
Why do computers use binary?

00:06:31.408 --> 00:06:32.387
3 reasons.

00:06:32.737 --> 00:06:34.048
It's easy to build.

00:06:34.048 --> 00:06:38.394
A circuit only has to tell 2 states apart, high voltage or low,

00:06:38.394 --> 00:06:42.257
which is far more reliable than telling 10 levels apart.

00:06:42.607 --> 00:06:44.560
It's simple to calculate with.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:48.727
The addition and multiplication tables have only 4 entries each.

00:06:49.077 --> 00:06:51.957
And it combines easily with logic.

00:06:51.957 --> 00:06:54.666
1 and 0 can mean true and false,

00:06:54.666 --> 00:06:58.477
so the same circuits do arithmetic and logic.

00:06:59.267 --> 00:07:01.276
Binary numbers get long,

00:07:01.276 --> 00:07:03.787
so we also use 2 bigger bases.

00:07:04.137 --> 00:07:08.044
Hexadecimal, or hex for short, has base 16.

00:07:08.044 --> 00:07:12.858
It needs 16 symbols, so after 0 to 9 it uses letters.

00:07:12.858 --> 00:07:19.217
The letter A means 10, B means 11, and so on, up to F, which means 15.

00:07:19.567 --> 00:07:23.127
Octal has base 8, and the symbols 0 to 7.

00:07:23.477 --> 00:07:26.187
Here they are side by side.

00:07:26.187 --> 00:07:30.301
Look where each system first writes 1, 0:

00:07:30.301 --> 00:07:35.117
at 2 in binary, at 8 in octal, and at 16 in hex.

00:07:35.467 --> 00:07:39.501
Python can write whole numbers in these bases too.

00:07:39.501 --> 00:07:45.390
A number that starts with '0b' is binary, '0o' is octal, and '0x' is hex.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:49.827
The functions `bin`, `oct`, and `hex` go the other way.

00:07:50.177 --> 00:07:52.126
And here's the trap.

00:07:52.126 --> 00:07:54.171
1, 0 isn't always 10.

00:07:54.171 --> 00:07:59.042
In binary it's 2. In octal it's 8. In hex it's 16.

00:07:59.042 --> 00:08:02.743
In any base, 10 means the base itself.

00:08:02.743 --> 00:08:06.737
So always know which base you're reading.

00:08:07.534 --> 00:08:08.974
Let's sum up.

00:08:09.324 --> 00:08:11.485
The CPU runs only machine code.

00:08:11.485 --> 00:08:16.503
Higher-level languages are faster to write, lower-level ones run faster,

00:08:16.503 --> 00:08:20.964
and the operating system manages the hardware for every program.

00:08:21.314 --> 00:08:26.794
In positional notation, each digit is multiplied by a power of the base.

00:08:27.144 --> 00:08:29.303
Computers use binary, base 2.

00:08:29.303 --> 00:08:32.504
Octal and hex are compact ways to write it.

00:08:32.854 --> 00:08:34.563
Here's one to try.

00:08:34.563 --> 00:08:37.316
Count from 0 to 10 in base 3.

00:08:37.316 --> 00:08:39.974
Which numbers need 3 digits?

00:08:40.524 --> 00:08:45.788
In part 3, we'll convert numbers between bases by hand, fractions included,

00:08:45.788 --> 00:08:48.244
and check every answer with Python.

